<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Sources and further reading</h2><ul class="essay-refs"><li>Israel, J. I. (1995) The Dutch Republic: Its Rise, Greatness and Fall, 1477–1806. Oxford University Press.</li><li>Price, J. L. (1998) The Dutch Republic in the Seventeenth Century. New York: St. Martin's Press.</li><li>Schama, S. (1987) The Embarrassment of Riches: An Interpretation of Dutch Culture in the Golden Age. New York: Alfred Knopf.</li><li>de Vries, J. and van der Woude, A. (2010) The First Modern Economy: Success, failure, and perseverance of the Dutch economy, 1500–1815. Cambridge University Press.</li><li>Helmers, H. J. and Janssen, G. H., eds. (2018) The Cambridge Companion to the Dutch Golden Age. Cambridge University Press.</li></ul><p class="soc-method">The shares are of the whole population, children included. Where a scholar has counted or estimated a rank, its share follows that work, named on the rank; where no count survives, the share is an estimate, marked est. and drawn dashed, made from what the rank was and did, from related figures and from the same rank in comparable societies, and its note says what it rests on. Population: &quot;around 1.5 million inhabitants&quot;, as the Dutch Republic article gives it, without a named estimate behind the figure.</p></section>
