e elite.","kid":"Rich merchant families ran the Dutch cities, and their men sat for life on the town councils.","rise":"Only men with enough wealth and long residence in the city could join, and an ambitious merchant family’s first step was a marriage into an old regent family. Everyone who held public office had to conform to the Reformed Church, and upheavals could purge a whole council: the Remonstrant regents in 1618, the Orangists in 1650, the followers of Johan de Witt in 1672.","why":"The regents (regenten) were the rulers of the Republic, the men who governed its cities and through them the provinces: not formally a hereditary class, but in practice patricians, from rich merchant families long…","people":[["johan-de-witt","Johan de Witt"]]},{"n":3,"lv":2,"band":"nobility","name":"The landed nobility","term":"ridderschap","lbl":"c. 0.05–0.2% (est.)","w":0.125,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/dutch-republic-3.jpg","you":"You hold land in one of the inland provinces and sit with the nobles who speak for the countryside in the States; to keep up your house you may marry your daughter to a wealthy merchant.","kid":"There were only a few nobles in the Dutch Republic, and many of them married into rich merchant families.","rise":"Birth made a noble, but wealthy merchants bought themselves into the nobility by becoming landowners and acquiring a coat of arms and a seal. This mixing of patricians and aristocrats was greatest in the second half of the century.","why":"The landed nobility counted for relatively little in the Republic.","people":[]},{"n":4,"lv":3,"band":"religious","name":"Ministers of the Reformed Church","term":"-","lbl":"c. 0.1–0.3% (est.)","w":0.2,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/synod-of-dort.jpg","you":"You preach in the public church, the only one whose members may hold office; your church condemned the Remonstrants at Dort and keeps a close watch on the lives of your town.","kid":"Ministers of the Reformed Church preached in plain churches without statues, and only church members could hold public office.","rise":"Ministers came from the affluent middle class. After the Synod of Dort banned the Remonstrant faith, those who denied predestination had no place in the public church.","why":"Calvinism was the state religion, and the Dutch Reformed Church the “public” or “privileged” church: anyone who wished to hold public office had to conform to it and take an oath.","people":[]},{"n":5,"lv":3,"band":"specialists","name":"The affluent middle class","term":"-","lbl":"c. 5–10% (est.)","w":7.5,"firm":1,"est":1,"pic":"/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/antonie-van-leeuwenhoek.jpg","you":"You are a lawyer in Leiden, trained at its university; you sit for the group portrait of your town’s militia company and buy a still life for your house.","kid":"Lawyers, doctors and small merchants could read, bought paintings and had their portraits painted.","rise":"Wealth and education opened the way: universities led to public office, and a rich family could hope to marry into the regents. Newcomers joined too: many of the Huguenots who came from France after 1685 were shopkeepers or scientists.","why":"After the aristocrats and patricians came the affluent middle class: Protestant ministers, lawyers, physicians, small merchants, industrialists and the clerks of the large state institutions.","people":[["hugo-grotius","Hugo Grotius"],["christiaan-huygens","Christiaan Huygens"],["antonie-van-leeuwenhoek","Antonie van Leeuwenhoek"]]},{"n":6,"lv":4,"band":"commoners","name":"Farmers, craftsmen and shopkeepers","term":"-","lbl":"About 38% (the rest, est.)","w":38.125,"firm":1,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/dutch-republic-6.jpg","you":"You farm land drained from a lake by windmills and sell your grain and dairy to the towns and the seafaring population.","kid":"Farmers, craftsmen and shopkeepers grew food, made goods and ran shops, and windmills pumped water off new farmland.","rise":"Status followed income, so a craftsman or shopkeeper who prospered could rise into the affluent middle class; a skilled tradesman earned about 200 guilders a year.","why":"Lower in status came the farmers, craft and tradesmen, shopkeepers and government bureaucrats.","people":[]},{"n":7,"lv":4,"band":"commoners","name":"Catholics","term":"-","lbl":"c. 18–23% (est.)","w":20.5,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/johannes-vermeer.jpg","you":"You hear Mass in a house that doubles as a church, a privilege your community has paid for, and live in your town’s Catholic quarter; no public office will ever be yours.","kid":"Catholics could pray only in hidden house churches and were not allowed to hold public office.","rise":"Birth into a Catholic family decided it, and the way to office lay only through conforming to the Reformed Church. Wealth and status belonged almost exclusively to Protestants.","why":"The Catholic Church had been more or less suppressed since the war with Spain began, and Catholic services were soon forbidden in every province.","people":[["johannes-vermeer","Johannes Vermeer"]]},{"n":8,"lv":4,"band":"commoners","name":"Jews","term":"-","lbl":"c. 0.2–0.6% (est.)","w":0.4,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/baruch-spinoza.jpg","you":"You are a Portuguese Jewish merchant in Amsterdam: you worship openly and trade on equal terms with Dutch merchants, but you can never be a citizen of the city or hold public office.","kid":"Jewish families who had fled Spain and Portugal could live and pray openly in Amsterdam.","rise":"Birth into the community decided it. A Sephardic merchant could grow as rich as any Dutch merchant, but Amsterdam’s citizenship stayed closed to him.","why":"The Republic drew Portuguese and Spanish Jews fleeing the Inquisitions of Iberia in the 16th and 17th centuries, and later poorer German Jews.","people":[["baruch-spinoza","Baruch Spinoza"]]},{"n":9,"lv":5,"band":"commoners","name":"Skilled labourers, servants and sailors","term":"-","lbl":"c. 20–30% (est.)","w":25,"firm":1,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/dutch-republic-9.jpg","you":"You came to Amsterdam from the Protestant lands of Germany and sail on a merchant ship to the Baltic; your wages are higher than at home, and so are your taxes.","kid":"Sailors, maids and workers earned more in the Dutch Republic than in most of Europe.","rise":"Work drew migrants from the Dutch countryside and from abroad, where many people were poor and unemployed. Since status followed income, better pay could lift a family.","why":"Below the craftsmen stood skilled labourers, maids, servants, sailors and others employed in the service trades.","people":[]},{"n":10,"lv":6,"band":"dependent","name":"Paupers","term":"-","lbl":"c. 5–10% (est.)","w":7.5,"firm":1,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/dutch-republic-10.jpg","you":"You left your village with nothing and beg in the streets of a Holland town, taking day labour when you can find it; the city gives no encouragement to newcomers from the countryside like you.","kid":"The poorest people begged or worked by the day, and the towns built almshouses for the old.","rise":"Poverty drove peasants off the land into the towns, where a beggar might find work as a day labourer; the cities did little for the dispossessed who came from the countryside.","why":"At the bottom of the pyramid stood the paupers: impoverished peasants, many of whom tried their luck in a city as beggars or day labourers.","people":[]}]}</script>
